            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines in the plane that satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines. Hence the required configuration does **not** exist.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Points that must be covered.**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the condition “\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)” yields the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Property of sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) can contain **at most two** points of \\(S\\).  \n   *Proof.* If a sunny line contained three points of \\(S\\), among those three there would be two with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, or the same sum \\(x+y\\) (pigeon‑hole principle on the three possible values of each quantity). The line would then be vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\n3. **The non‑sunny line must contain at least two points.**  \n   If the unique non‑sunny line \\(L_{0}\\) contained at most one point of \\(S\\), the remaining five points would have to be covered by the two sunny lines. By Lemma 1 each sunny line can cover at most two points, so together 