            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_n|\n   =\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family of diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is non‑sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the integer solutions of \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(x,y\\ge 1\\) are exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) satisfying \\(a+b=c\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}L_c\\cap\\mathcal{P}_n=\\mathcal{P}_n .\n\\]\n\nThe lines in (2) are distinct because they have different constant terms.  \nThus for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is always attainable}.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-1 = \\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}\\,(x-1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i}{n+1-i}\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{n+1-i}>0,\\qquad\n\\frac{i}{n+1-i}\\neq -1,\n\\]\n\nso none of the lines \\(S_i\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then the slopes in (4) are different, so the lines are distinct.\n\nWe now show that the family \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  Put  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-1,\\qquad j:=a-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n0\\le i\\le n-1,\\qquad 0\\le j\\le n-1,\\qquad i+j\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(j\\le n-i-1\\), i.e. \\(j\\le n-i-1\\).  Multiplying by \\(\\frac{i+1}{n-i}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i+1}{\\,n-i\\,}j\\le i .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) are integers, and the left‑hand side of (6) is also an integer because the denominator divides the numerator times \\(j\\) (this follows from \\(j\\le n-i-1\\)).  Hence equality must hold:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i+1}{\\,n-i\\,}j = i .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nRe‑writing (7) yields  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = \\frac{i+1}{\\,n-i\\,}(a-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of the line \\(S_{i+1}\\) from (4).  Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{i+1}\\).\n\nSince the choice of \\((a,b)\\) was arbitrary, every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\).  Consequently we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all required points, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=n\\text{ is always attainable}.}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Remarks on the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe two constructions above settle the extreme cases \\(k=0\\) and \