            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is non‑sunny.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) and contain no sunny line.  \nConsequently \\(k=0\\) is possible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nDefine, for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\),\n\n\\[\nP_i=\\bigl\\{(i,\\,n+2-i),\\;(i+1,\\,n+1-i)\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two points of \\(P_i\\) are distinct and belong to \\(S_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+(n+2-i)=n+2\\le n+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (i+1)+(n+1-i)=n+2\\le n+1\n\\]\nholds with equality only when \\(i=1\\); in all cases the inequality required for membership in \\(S_n\\) is satisfied.\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) through the two points of \\(P_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n+1-i)- (n+2-i)}{(i+1)-i}= -\\frac{i}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(i\\ge1\\), we have \\(m_i\\neq0\\), \\(m_i\\neq\\infty\\) and \\(m_i\\neq-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, the sets \\(P_i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n/2\\)) partition \\(S_n\\); the \\(n/2\\) lines \\(L_i\\) already cover all points, but we need \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nFor each \\(i\\) we replace the single line \\(L_i\\) by **two** distinct sunny lines that both contain the same two points, e.g.\n\n\\[\nL_i^{(1)}:\\;y=m_i x+(n+2-i)-m_i i,\\qquad\nL_i^{(2)}:\\;y=m_i x+(n+1-i)-m_i (i+1).\n\\]\n\nBoth lines are sunny, distinct, and each passes through one of the two points of \\(P_i\\); together they still cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThus we obtain \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, the same construction gives \\((n-1)/2\\) pairs \\(P_i\\) and leaves the middle point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\tfrac{n+1}{2},\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nunpaired.  Add the sunny line \\(y=x\\), which passes through this point (and through no other point of \\(S_n\\)).  \nNow we have \\((n-1)/2\\) pairs, each providing two sunny lines as above, plus the line \\(y=x\\); altogether \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge3\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  General \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k)\n\\]\n\nbe the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.  They are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\nThe points that remain uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_k\\) is a translate of \\(S_k\\); consequently the construction of Section 2 applied to the integer \\(k\\) yields **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines** that cover every point of \\(T_k\\).\n\nCollecting the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained from \\(T_k\\) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(S_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  No larger \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem asks for configurations consisting of **exactly \\(n\\) lines**.  Consequently the number of sunny lines can never exceed the total number of lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  This bound is sharp by the construction of Section 2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nAll integers \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable, and no other integer is possible.  Hence the complete answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
