            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three\nlines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{y}:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A covering with no sunny line  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); consequently it lies\non the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  The lines \\(H_j\\) are parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, hence they are **not** sunny.  Therefore a configuration with\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A family of sunny lines that together with a few horizontals\ncovers all of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = -\\frac{i}{\\,n-i+1\\,}\\,x + \\frac{n+1}{\\,n-i+1\\,}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(n-i+1,\\;i)\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nsatisfy the equation of \\(L_i\\); hence both belong to \\(L_i\\).\nBecause the slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{n-i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\),\neach \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nD_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nis exactly \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_n\\}\\); the set  \n\n\\[\nD_2=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n\\}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nis \\(\\{Q_1,\\dots ,Q_{n-1}\\}\\).  Consequently\n\n* every point of \\(D_1\\) lies on a unique line \\(L_i\\) (the one with\n  \\(i=a\\) if \\(a\\le t\\), otherwise it is covered by the horizontal line\n  \\(y=n+1-a\\) that we shall keep);\n* every point of \\(D_2\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n-a\\).\n\nHence the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_t=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-t\\,\\}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\ncovers **all** points of \\(S_n\\).  The set \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) consists of\nexactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-t\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines; the\nlines are distinct because the slopes of the \\(L_i\\) are pairwise different\nand none of them is horizontal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Realisation of every value of \\(k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we choose \\(t=k\\) in the\nconstruction of §2.  When \\(k=0\\) we obtain the purely horizontal covering\nof §1; when \\(k=n\\) we keep only the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) and no\nhorizontal line.  In every case we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Exhaustiveness\n\nObviously no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines because\nonly \\(n\\) lines are allowed.  The constructions above show that every\ninteger between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.  Hence the set of admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\\).**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
