            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The statement for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\) (the third line is unused but allowed) cover all points and none of them is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): the lines  \n  \\[\n  y=1,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n  \\]\n  cover \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (first two lines) and \\((1,2)\\) (the third line).  Exactly one line (\\(y=-2x+5\\)) is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): the lines  \n  \\[\n  y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=3\n  \\]\n  are distinct; the first two have slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (both sunny) and together they contain the three points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac43\n  \\]\n  each pass through a different point of \\(S_3\\); consequently every point is covered and all three lines are sunny.\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive step\n\nAssume that for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**Induction hypothesis**)  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set  \n> \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\nWe shall construct from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) two families of \\(n+1\\) lines that satisfy the same two conditions for the set \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Two auxiliary lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_{n+1}:\\;y=n+1 ,\\qquad\nT_{n+1}:\\;y=-2x+(n+3).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*The line \\(H_{n+1}\\) is horizontal, hence not sunny.*  \n*The line \\(T_{n+1}\\) has slope \\(-2\\); therefore it is sunny.*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points covered by the auxiliary lines\n\n*Points with second coordinate \\(n+1\\).*  \nThe only point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) having \\(b=n+1\\) is \\((1,n+1)\\); it lies on \\(H_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Points on the outer diagonal \\(a+b=n+2\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\) and \\(a+b=n+2\\), then \\(b=n+2-a\\) and\n\n\\[\n-2a+(n+3)=n+3-2a=n+2-a=b .\n\\]\n\nHence every point of the diagonal \\(a+b=n+2\\) belongs to \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*All remaining points of \\(S_{n+1}\\).*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) already belongs to \\(S_n\\) and is therefore covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (induction hypothesis).\n\nConsequently each point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) lies on at least one line of the following two families:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{H_{n+1}\\}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{T_{n+1}\\}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Verification of the families\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nBoth families contain exactly \\(|\\mathcal L_{n,k}|+1=n+1\\) lines.\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe added line \\(H_{n+1}\\) (horizontal) is not parallel to any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) whose slope is either \\(0\\) (if such a line already existed) or different from \\(0\\); in any case it is a new line.  \nThe added line \\(T_{n+1}\\) has slope \\(-2\\), a value that does not occur among the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) because all sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) have slopes different from \\(-2\\) (otherwise two distinct sunny lines would be parallel, contradicting the requirement that the \\(n\\) lines be distinct). Hence \\(T_{n+1}\\) is also new.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nIn \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) the newly added line \\(H_{n+1}\\) is not sunny, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).  \nIn \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) the newly added line \\(T_{n+1}\\) is sunny, therefore the number of sunny lines becomes \\(k+1\\).\n\nThus, assuming the induction hypothesis for \\(n\\), we have produced configurations for \\(n+1\\) with exactly \\(k\\) and exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines, for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) supplies configurations for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nApplying the inductive step repeatedly, we obtain for every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Upper bound and final answer\n\nObviously a set of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines; hence \\(k\\le n\\) is necessary.  \nThe construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
