\(S_n\\) is covered.  \n\nThus we have exhibited a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are sunny, that covers every point of \\(S_n\\). Consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### Remarks on the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nThe constructions above settle the two extreme cases.  \nThe earlier answer attempted to obtain the intermediate values by replacing a diagonal line \\(x+y=n+2\\) with the single sunny line \\(y=-2x+(n+3)\\); this replacement is invalid because the line \\(y=-2x+(n+3)\\) contains only the point \\((1,n+2)\\) of the set \\(S_{n+1}\\) and fails to cover the other points with \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \n\nA correct method for the intermediate values would have to replace a collection of non‑sunny diagonal lines by a collection of sunny lines in such a way that the total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\) and every point remains covered. Designing such a replacement for an arbitrary \\(k\\) is non‑trivial and was not achieved in the present work. Consequently the problem of determining whether **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable remains open within this answer.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
