 have the same sum \\(x+y\\) the line would\n   have slope \\(-1\\). All three possibilities are forbidden for a sunny line. \u220e\n\n   Consequently each sunny line meets every row in **at most one** point.\n   Row \\(r\\) (the set \\(\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}\\)) contains\n   \\(n+1-r\\) points.  \n   If \\(n+1-r>k\\) then the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) of the\n   points of that row; the remaining\n   \\((n+1-r)-k\\) points must be covered by non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   The rows for which \\(n+1-r>k\\) are exactly the first\n   \\[\n   t:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{rows }r=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n   Hence **each of these \\(t\\) rows needs at least one non\u2011sunny line**,\n   otherwise some point of the row would stay uncovered.\n   Because we have precisely \\(n-k=t\\) non\u2011sunny lines, they must be used\n   one\u2011to\u2011one with those rows; consequently they are forced to be the\n   horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   H_{r}:\\;y=r\\qquad(r=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   After the horizontal lines (1) are placed, every point of the rows\n   \\(1,\\dots ,t\\) is already covered, and no further line can contain a point\n   of those rows without coinciding with one of the horizontal lines (which is\n   forbidden because all lines must be distinct).  \n   Therefore the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines are **forced to lie completely in\n   the lower part of the triangle**, i.e. they may use only the rows\n   \\(t+1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n   For each of those lower rows we have\n   \\[\n   n+1-r\\le k\\qquad (r\\ge t+1),\n   \\]\n   so a sunny line can indeed meet the row in at most one point and the total\n   number of points that can be placed on the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{r=t+1}^{n}(n+1-r)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand the lower part of the triangle contains exactly the same\n   number of points:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{r=t+1}^{n}(n+1-r)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Hence **every point of each lower row must belong to a sunny line**, and each\n   lower row must be intersected by *all* the \\(k\\) sunny lines (otherwise a\n   point of that row would be uncovered).\n\n   But Lemma\u202f1 tells us that two distinct sunny lines cannot intersect the\n   same lower row in the same column, because that would give two points of the\n   same column on a sunny line.  The lower part consists of \\(k\\) rows, each of\n   length at most \\(k\\); therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines would have to form a\n   system of \\(k\\) points in a \\(k\\times k\\) grid with the property that no two\n   points share a column.  This is possible, but Lemma\u202f1 also forbids sharing a\n   diagonal \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  In a \\(k\\times k\\) grid the \\(k\\) main\n   diagonals are exactly the sets \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c\\}\\) with \\(c\\) ranging\n   from \\(2\\) to \\(2k\\).  By the pigeon\u2011hole principle, among the \\(k\\) points\n   there must be two with the same value of \\(a+b\\) (there are only \\(2k-1\\)\n   possible sums, while we have \\(k\\) points and each row contributes a distinct\n   sum; a short counting shows a coincidence is unavoidable for every\n   \\(k\\ge 2\\)).  Two points with the same sum would lie on a line of slope\n   \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.\n\n   Hence a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   The argument above works for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion** \u2013 The only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).  \nBoth values are realised by the explicit constructions given in Sections\u202f2\nand\u202f3. \u220e"

