t \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is exhaustive.\n\nThe construction works for every \\(k\\) in the stated range, and the lemma\nguarantees that the sunny part always exists; therefore the answer is\ncomplete.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled\ntriangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\nAll other slopes are allowed.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\nFix a non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\).  \nPlace the following \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nH_{r}:\\;y=r\\qquad (r=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(H_{r}\\) contains exactly the points of the \\(r\\)-th row of the\ntriangle, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the union of the lines (1) covers **all** points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\nDelete those covered rows.  The remaining uncovered points are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge n-k+1,\\;\na+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate \\(\\mathcal Q_{k}\\) downward by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{k}-(0,n-k)=\\{(a,b')\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1,\\;a+b'\\le k+1\\}\n   =\\mathcal P_{k}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal Q_{k}\\) is a copy of the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_{k}\\) shifted\nupwards by \\(n-k\\) units.\n\nHence, after fixing the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines, the original problem is\nequivalent to the following sub‑problem:\n\n> **Sub‑problem.**  For a given integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) find \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n> lines that together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{k}\\).\n\nIf we can solve the sub‑problem for every \\(k\\), then adding the horizontal\nlines (1) gives a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which precisely\n\\(k\\) are sunny.  Therefore the existence of such a configuration for a\ngiven \\(k\\) is equivalent to the solvability of the sub‑problem.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma – covering \\(\\mathcal P_{k}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma.**  \n*For every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose\nunion contains all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{k}\\).*\n\n*Proof by explicit construction.*  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{k+1}{i+1}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{i}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\); this number is never\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for any positive integer \\(i\\), hence each\n\\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  The lines are distinct because their slopes are\ndifferent.\n\nWe claim that every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{k}\\) lies on at least one of\nthe lines (2).  Since \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\),\nthe rational number  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\frac{b- \\frac{k+1}{i+1}}{a}\n\\]\n\nequals \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) for exactly one index \\(i\\) in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\nIndeed, solving the equation  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i}{i+1}a + \\frac{k+1}{i+1}\n\\]\n\nfor \\(i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ni = \\frac{(k+1)-b}{a-b}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are positive integers with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), the right‑hand side\nis a positive integer not exceeding \\(k\\); consequently there exists a unique\n\\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) satisfying the equality, and for that \\(i\\) the point\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{i}\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_{i}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{k}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Completion of the construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (1).  \nApply the Lemma to the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_{k}\\) and obtain the sunny lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) given by (2).  Finally shift each of these \\(k\\) lines\nupwards by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\); the shifted lines are\n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_{i}:\\; y-n+k = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{k+1}{i+1},\n\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nor equivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{k+1}{i+1}+n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe shift does not change the slope, therefore each \\(\\widetilde L_{i}\\) is still\nsunny, and the \\(\\widetilde L_{i}\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n\nBy construction:\n\n* the horizontal lines (1) cover all points with \\(b\\le n-k\\);\n* the shifted sunny lines \\(\\widetilde L_{i}\\) cover, after the upward shift,\n  exactly the points with \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).\n\nConsequently the union of the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_{1},\\dots ,\\widetilde L_{k}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains **every** point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(\\widetilde L_{i}\\) are sunny, while the \\(n-k\\) horizontals are not.  Hence\nfor the chosen \\(k\\) a required configuration exists.\n\nBecause we never used more than \\(n\\) lines, the construction works for **any**\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  The case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by taking only the\n\\(n\\) diagonals \\(x+y=s\\;(s=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\); the case \\(k=n\\) is obtained by\ntaking the \\(n\\) sunny lines supplied by the Lemma (no horizontals are used).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Exhaustiveness  \n\nThe problem asks for *all* non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  Since we have exhibited a construction\nfor every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and obviously we cannot have more than\n\\(n\\) sunny line