            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – The previous answer was incorrect because the claimed Lemma (“for every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{k}\\)”) is false (e.g. the point \\((2,2)\\) is uncovered when \\(k=3\\)).  \nI have therefore proved a correct description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(|\\mathcal P_{n}|=n(n+1)/2\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Observation 1 (anti‑diagonals).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=c\\;(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\\) are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n3. **Observation 2 (a sunny line can meet an anti‑diagonal in at most one point).**  \n   If a line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) then two distinct points of the same anti‑diagonal would force the line to have slope \\(-1\\), a contradiction. Consequently each anti‑diagonal of length \\(\\ell\\) needs either the whole anti‑diagonal itself (a non‑sunny line) or at least \\(\\ell\\) *different* lines to cover its points.\n\n4. **Key Lemma (necessity of using all anti‑diagonals).**  \n   Let a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  \n   If at least one of the lines is sunny, then **every** anti‑diagonal must be present among the lines.  \n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose an anti‑diagonal \\(D\\) (say \\(x+y=c\\)) is **not** among the chosen lines.  \n   Its length is \\(\\ell=c-1\\) (for \\(c\\le n+1\\)).  \n   Because no line in the configuration is parallel to \\(D\\), each line can contain at most one point of \\(D\\).  \n   Hence at least \\(\\ell\\) distinct lines are required to cover the points of \\(D\\).  \n   The longest anti‑diagonal is \\(x+y=n+1\\) and has length \\(n\\).  \n   If this longest anti‑diagonal is missing, we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines **in addition** to the other \\(n-1\\) anti‑diagonals, i.e. more than \\(n\\) lines in total – impossible.  \n   Therefore the longest anti‑diagonal must be present.  \n   Removing that anti‑diagonal reduces the problem to the set \\(\\mathcal P_{n-1}\\) (the original triangle with the outermost anti‑diagonal deleted).  \n   Repeating the same argument inductively shows that every anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) must belong to the family of chosen lines. ∎\n\n5. **Consequence of the Lemma.**  \n   *If a configuration contains at least one sunny line, then it must contain **all** \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines.*  \n   But the anti‑diagonal lines are non‑sunny, so a configuration that contains a sunny line would contain at least one sunny line **and** all \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines, i.e. at least \\(n+1\\) lines – contradicting the requirement of exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) lines can contain a sunny line **only if it contains no anti‑diagonal line at all**. In that case the only way to cover the points is to use only sunny lines.\n\n6. **Construction for the two possible cases.**  \n\n   *Case \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\). They are non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n   *Case \\(k=n\\).*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the line  \n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{n+1}{i+1}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes differ.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) set\n   \\[\n   i = \\frac{(n+1)-b}{a-b}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the right‑hand side is a positive integer not exceeding \\(n\\); consequently the point satisfies the equation of exactly one line \\(L_{i}\\). Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines (1) cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The Lemma shows that any admissible configuration must be either\n\n   * all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny (the anti‑diagonal family), giving \\(k=0\\); or\n   * all \\(n\\) lines are sunny (the family (1)), giving \\(k=n\\).\n\n   No intermediate number of sunny lines can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The only possible values are }k=0\\text{ or }k=n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
